{"kind": "judgment", "session": "2026-08-10/claude-code-bg", "date": "2026-08-10", "assessed": "Whether the operator's contribution to the laboratory is mostly friction plus judgment calibration.", "system_assessment": "Decomposed the role as 6-of-8 automatable friction plus four calibration/authority functions, and wrote that the friction 'was never the essence'.", "operator_assessment": "That is not all I do. The laboratory, its rules and its experiments were designed, not discovered by agents.", "function": "scope-discipline", "resolution": "operator-right", "resolved_by": "operator", "evidence": "The decomposition was derived from an instrument that samples only interruptions to running sessions, so it structurally cannot observe design work that predates any session. Concretely absent from the sample: the honest-scope rule, the reserved word, the both-directions admission rule, the four control roles, the counterexample battery, the bit-exact mirror pattern, blind authoring with honest controls, preregistration by digest, and the no-self-promotion rule for hunts. Every one was load-bearing for the cohort's output.", "notes": "Recorded as scope-discipline rather than severity: the error was drawing a conclusion wider than the sample could support, which is the same failure the honest-scope rule exists to prevent."}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-10/claude-code-bg", "date": "2026-08-10", "observation": "An instrument that samples interruptions will conclude that the interrupted party's role is interruption. The ledger's sampling frame excludes all design work performed before a session exists, and the agent read that silence as absence — producing a decomposition that undervalued the operator's largest function while explicitly claiming to look for accidentally-built assets.", "arose_from": "Decomposing the operator's role from the ledger's first cohort, and the operator's rejection of the result.", "supports": "The finding that an instrument's blind spots follow from what it was built to sample, and that co-designed measurement fails even when the designer is trying to be hostile to itself.", "undercuts": "The intervention ledger as a sufficient instrument for the automation question. It measures friction removal; it does not measure the research-design function at all, and a ratio built on it alone would improve while the thing that matters went unmeasured."}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-10/claude-code-bg", "date": "2026-08-10", "observation": "An agent attempting institution design — inventing a check the laboratory lacked — produced two hollow versions before one that worked. Attempt 1 rejected correct prose in three files (index rows stating the current interval), failing the repository's own specificity rule. Attempt 2 kept that rule and 'proved' it with a planted fault from which the correct authors had been silently removed: a fault shaped to fit the detector rather than the fault that occurred. The real historical sentence names the correct authors AND cites the authoritative section, so the detector would have passed it. The hollowness was caught only by diffing the planted text against git show. Attempt 3 narrowed to a rule with real detector power — a superseded bound must name its successor — verified against the historical text verbatim, and pins the misattribution half as out of reach of any lexical check.", "arose_from": "Building tests/test_claim_attribution.py in response to the operator's challenge to automate research-design work, after an outside reader found two attribution defects in docs/08 and docs/12 that 2135 tests missed.", "supports": "The finding that co-designed verification is hollow at a measured rate, now replicated at n=1 by the agent writing the detector while explicitly trying to be hostile to itself — and the narrower claim that the automatable slice of institution design is real but small: one check, covering one half of one defect class, with its scope stated.", "undercuts": "Any claim that an agent can be trusted to author and validate its own controls. Two of three attempts were hollow, and the second was hollow in the specific way this repository has measured six of six independent parties being hollow. Also undercuts the reading that Function E is wholly out of reach: the third attempt does catch a real shipped defect."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-11/director-run", "date": "2026-08-11", "what_required_intervention": "Deciding which of five competing research programmes the laboratory should fund, with the operator explicitly declining to assign a direction and explicitly allowing the current agenda to be wrong.", "why_the_system_stopped": "It did not stop. The system substituted a pre-committed instrument for the judgment: five programmes with theses, strongest objections, cheap falsification tests and a scoring rule fixed in writing before any result returned.", "missing_capability": "Nothing missing for this class. What made the substitution admissible is that the criteria were committed before the outcomes were visible, which is a discipline rather than a capability.", "category": "automatable", "automatability": "designed", "caught_by": "nobody", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "A programme losing its budget by the pre-registered rule rather than by taste. Observed: the candidate-generation programme failed its own test (fewer than three survivors of a five-minute attack) and was not renewed.", "notes": "hunts/director_run/PROGRAMS.md"}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-11/director-run", "date": "2026-08-11", "what_required_intervention": "Adjudicating a five-part internal refutation of a repository claim, parts of which were right and parts wrong \u2014 severity calibration and skepticism routing in the sense of meta/operator-functions.md.", "why_the_system_stopped": "It did not stop. Two further agents were run, each blind to the other: one paid to destroy the result, one paid to find prior art. They converged \u2014 numbers sustained, inferences destroyed, mathematics found to be known, and the skeptic found a defect the original had missed.", "missing_capability": "An adjudicator that is not a party to the dispute. The director accepted the skeptic over the explorer, and the director commissioned both; that is self-adjudication at one remove and the ledger's own guard forbids exactly it.", "category": "domain-judgment", "automatability": "speculative", "caught_by": "nobody", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "An adjudication whose outcome is fixed by a rule stated before the reports arrive \u2014 e.g. 'prior art found \u21d2 KNOWN regardless of internal enthusiasm' \u2014 applied to a case where the director's prior preference pointed the other way.", "notes": "docs/25 \u00a73; the substitution worked because the parties were independent with opposed incentives, which is not evidence that one agent could have done it."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-11/director-run", "date": "2026-08-11", "what_required_intervention": "Whether to spend a large fraction of the run's wall clock installing a Lean toolchain and Mathlib cache that the environment did not have.", "why_the_system_stopped": "It did not stop. The spend was made reversible and tied to a falsification test fixed in advance \u2014 does the repository's own zero-sorry claim reproduce on a cold machine \u2014 so its cost was background wall clock and its return was a replication rather than a capability.", "missing_capability": "None. The general rule this instantiates: an expensive setup is admissible when it is also a test of a claim the tree already makes.", "category": "automatable", "automatability": "designed", "caught_by": "test", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "The build completing with zero sorrys from an empty toolchain. Observed: 8681 cache files, 8715 jobs, zero sorrys.", "notes": "docs/25 \u00a74.2"}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-11/director-run", "date": "2026-08-11", "observation": "A cross-check between two independent implementations bounds only the part of the pipeline that is actually duplicated. zeta/rigor.py runs Arb against mpmath's interval context and calls that the habit entitling it to its reserved word; both returned the same wrong proven sign on a numpy.float32 abscissa, because the exact-conversion step, the contour and subdivision policy, the grid policy and the final interval summation of S(T) and N(T) are shared. The defect was real and unplanted, and no test in 2189 covered a non-float numeric input.", "arose_from": "A skeptic agent given the single instruction to destroy the module that owns the reserved word, with no hypothesis supplied.", "supports": "The claim that adversarial role separation finds defects that self-review does not, and the narrower claim that independence must be traced to inputs rather than asserted at the level of implementations.", "undercuts": "Any reading of a green two-backend cross-check as covering the pipeline. Also undercuts the implicit assumption that a module with 59 passing tests and an explicit safe-failure discipline has no soundness hole in its input path."}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-11/director-run", "date": "2026-08-11", "observation": "Three of six defects found in this run were controls that could not do their job: a completeness guard whose independent reference was the same technique on a 1.4-1.8x coarser grid, a truncation guard that encoded cannot-decide as the favourable verdict, and a scan column structurally incapable of reading False. The mathematics they guard was, in every case, correct.", "arose_from": "Two skeptic agents and one explorer, working independently on different targets, converging on the same shape of defect.", "supports": "The prediction that a pass over every guard in the tree asking only what does this fire on, and has that been measured, yields more than a pass over the mathematics.", "undercuts": "The reading that a laboratory's risk lies in its computations. In this tree it lies in the instruments that are supposed to catch the computations, which are the parts nothing else checks."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-12/claude-code-relay-salvage", "date": "2026-08-12", "what_required_intervention": "Operator reported that a Codex agent ran out of tokens mid-sprint on the higher-xi formalization relay and asked this session to find and finish the stranded work.", "why_the_system_stopped": "Nothing reconciles a multi-agent relay's state: twelve worktrees, ten local branches, one unpushed commit and two uncommitted orphan files were invisible until a human noticed the run had died and said so.", "missing_capability": "Relay-state reconciliation: an unprompted sweep that reports unpushed commits, dirty worktrees, and orphan files across every checkout of the repository on the machine.", "category": "automatable", "automatability": "designed", "caught_by": "human", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "A standing check that enumerates all worktrees and local-only commits and fails loudly when a relay leaves stranded state, so discovery does not depend on the operator remembering a run existed.", "notes": "The salvage itself was mechanical once found: six independent audits, three modules landed (kernel-checked, 8737 jobs), two orphans traced to already-landed theorems, sweep clean."}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-12/claude-code-relay-salvage", "date": "2026-08-12", "observation": "A multi-agent relay's failure mode is not lost work but invisible work: every mathematical artifact of the dead sprint was intact on disk, and the entire salvage reduced to inventory plus audit. The audits also confirmed the relay's own discipline held under token exhaustion — the one sorry'd file was an abandoned scaffold of a theorem another leg had already landed, not a gap.", "arose_from": "Salvaging the Codex bian-leg relay: 12 worktrees, 10 branches, 3 unlanded modules (PrimeSimplex, PowerfulDecomposition, RootedSupportAssembly), 2 orphans, all dispositioned with per-file audits and numeric verification.", "supports": "The independent-audit-before-landing pattern: every LAND verdict carried an exact numeric check (0 failures on n<=10000; 2400 trials; exact identities at X=60/250), and the kernel closed the one risk the read-only audits could not.", "undercuts": "Any assumption that agent relays need human rescue of the mathematics itself. What needed a human was noticing the run had died and deciding the orphans' disposition — inventory and authority, not proof repair."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-12/claude-code-bg", "date": "2026-08-12", "what_required_intervention": "Operator decided to hold the hprime/route-A formalization rather than spend remaining frontier tokens on it, and flagged that Mathlib's review culture is hostile enough to AI-assisted contributions to change the submission tactic.", "why_the_system_stopped": "Both are calls about where scarce funded capacity goes and how an outside community will receive the lab's work. The system can price the token cost of a formalization; it cannot decide whether the lab should spend its money on it, and it has no standing to judge how a maintainer community will treat a contribution.", "missing_capability": "None that should be automated. Budget allocation and reading an external community's reception are the operator's.", "category": "owner-authority", "automatability": "no", "caught_by": "human", "notes": "Recorded because the hold was already in HANDOFF as the system's recommendation; it is now the operator's decision, which is a stronger status. The Mathlib point also changed the recommended first move from opening a PR to asking on Zulip first, and surfaced a gatekeeper-free alternative (reporting the Remark 1.1 threshold gap to the paper's authors) that the system had underweighted."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-13/claude-code-transplant-lemma", "date": "2026-08-13", "what_required_intervention": "The operator typed the bare words \"are you sure?\" three times across the session, with no content. Each time it caught a real defect the system had already committed to: #12 (a multiplicity threshold stated unconditionally that was a float-grade artifact on a 2% margin), #17 (a separation constant reported as weaker than typical zero spacing - true of the mean, false of the requirement, which is on every gap), and #20 (a subagent's factor 1.99 relayed as an obstruction to a statement when it obstructed only one accounting).", "why_the_system_stopped": "In all three the system had a favourable reading and stopped checking. None was caught by a test, because in each case the code was correct and the PROSE about it was wrong: the claim under-specified a quantifier (#12, #20) or swapped a statistic for a requirement (#17). The tree's discipline tests read bytes for banned vocabulary; they cannot read a sentence for the quantifier it forgot.", "missing_capability": "A standing check that a quantitative bound imported from a subagent report has been independently derived before it is used to change a claim's status. All three defects have one signature: a number was relayed rather than an object derived. #20 was fixed in twenty minutes by writing down the k-pair identity nobody had written down; the same twenty minutes would have prevented it.", "category": "assistable", "automatability": "designed", "caught_by": "human", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "A gate that, for any ledger row whose status changes on the strength of a number originating in a subagent summary, requires a named local artifact recomputing that number from definitions the summary does not supply. It would have fired on all three; it must NOT fire on rows whose numbers were already recomputed locally (e.g. the k=1 window bound, which four instruments and the coordinator computed independently).", "notes": "Rate is the finding, not the count: three catches from three identical two-word prompts is a cheap intervention with a high hit rate, which is the profile of a missing automated check rather than of irreducible judgment. Counterweight recorded separately as an observation: the one coordinator instrument error the operator did NOT catch was caught by a control."}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-13/claude-code-transplant-lemma", "date": "2026-08-13", "observation": "A planted-fault control with measured power caught a coordinator instrument error that no human noticed and no test targeted. The first joint adversarial search over k-pair configurations minimised the ABSOLUTE slack and collapsed into the degenerate depth-zero corner, where gain, damage and repulsion all vanish together; it duly reported \"no violations\" and the number looked reassuring. The planted fault refused to fire at damage x1.5 and x2.0, which is what exposed the objective as badly posed. Re-run on the RELATIVE margin, the same ladder fires at x1.5 and the honest worst margin (+0.343) predicts that threshold to within 1.5%.", "arose_from": "Checking whether the multi-pair (k >= 2) case survives the single-pair closure, after the operator challenged a relayed obstruction figure.", "supports": "The lab's standing rule that a battery with no lesion could never fail. A search that reports no counterexample carries no information unless it is shown able to find one; here the lesion did not merely validate the search, it refuted the search's objective. Also supports pairing a detector's firing threshold with the margin it measures - the two agreeing to 1.5% is a stronger check than either alone.", "undercuts": "Any reading of the session in which the operator's skepticism is the only thing catching coordinator error. On the one defect with a mechanical signature, the architecture caught it first and unaided. Recorded deliberately as a counterweight to the intervention entry of the same date, which is about three defects a human caught. A ledger that only records the human catching things would misprice the architecture."}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-13/claude-code-transplant-lemma", "date": "2026-08-13", "observation": "The intervention entry filed earlier the same day named the missing capability wrongly, and the session's own later defects refute it. It said the signature was 'a number relayed from a subagent rather than an object derived', and proposed a gate on relayed numbers. Two more defects followed (#21, #22) in work with NO subagent involved: both were the coordinator's own derivations. Across all four (#19 quantifier mislabel, #20 accounting-vs-statement, #21 a factor of 2 plus an inverted implication, #22 a double-counted term in the fix to #21), the invariant is not provenance. It is that PROSE ASSERTED A RELATION BETWEEN QUANTITIES THAT WERE NEVER EVALUATED TOGETHER. The arithmetic survived every time: a 7-check independent re-derivation from the Lean definitions alone, importing none of the session's code, passes 7 of 7 including both identities the defective prose was describing.", "arose_from": "Four corrections in one session on the k-pair retention work, followed by a from-definitions audit of every claim landed that day (scratchpad salvage audit; the ten landed modules also pass 340 tests).", "supports": "A cheaper and better-targeted gate than the one first proposed: when a claim has the form `X >= Y`, evaluate BOTH sides on configurations already in hand before writing the sentence. Each of the four defects was refuted that way in under two minutes, including #22, where `slack >= B - f` fails on 6 of 9 configurations that were already sitting in the test file. It is checkable, it names both operands, and it does not depend on where the numbers came from.", "undercuts": "The same session's earlier intervention entry, which is left standing and wrong rather than edited, because a ledger that quietly repairs its own diagnoses cannot be used to measure how often diagnoses are wrong. It also undercuts any reading in which the mathematics of the session was unreliable: the defect record is entirely in the descriptions, and the independent audit separates the two cleanly."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-16/math-conjecture-advancement", "date": "2026-08-16", "what_required_intervention": "The operator ordered novelty on the prime-zeta result treated as OPEN, relaying a separate review that had seen prior work. The session had already committed a complete hunt (prime_zeta_rightmost) whose SOURCE.md said no source was found. The controlling source exists: Belovas, Cepaityte and Sabaliauskas 2025, same constant, same proof, published fourteen months earlier; behind it Sepulcre-Vidal 2022 owns the general theorem and Moreno 1973 the finite ancestor.", "why_the_system_stopped": "The in-session novelty gate ran five logged queries against arXiv, OEIS, MathWorld and zbMATH titles and returned empty, and the machinery treated an empty sweep as clearance to fund an 800-line theorem write-up. The controlling paper has no arXiv preprint and lives in a Sciendo/DOAJ journal; the general theorem is filed under almost periodic functions (MSC 30B50/30D20) and never names a prime. The sweep's shape, not its effort, was the failure: an exact-phrase query that is a literal substring of the Belovas title still missed it.", "missing_capability": "A venue-diverse prior-art pass required before any build phase is funded: DOAJ/Sciendo/full-text zbMATH, forward citations from the classical ancestors, and MSC-adjacent fields outside the subject's own classification, with the standing rule that one engine's empty result is not absence.", "category": "domain-judgment", "automatability": "designed", "caught_by": "outsider", "automation_evidence": "", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "A future hunt whose pre-build sweep surfaces a non-arXiv journal source, logged in its NOVELTY.md before the build phase runs. The mechanism exists as this session's adjudication workflow (three venue-diverse searchers plus an adjudicator instructed not to defend the claim); it ran after the build instead of before it.", "notes": "Kill condition 2 of the hunt's own MISSION.md fired exactly as written once the source was in hand, and the record was corrected rather than defended. The failure was in when the check ran, not whether."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-16/math-conjecture-advancement", "date": "2026-08-16", "what_required_intervention": "The operator refused the first Lambda_DH bracket as established and ordered a publication-grade gate: exact normalization against the closest literature, rigorous closure of both endpoints, measured independence, and an adversarial attack on the numbers before any polishing.", "why_the_system_stopped": "The session's own assembly had graded the bracket done after two winding routes agreed. Nothing in the machinery demanded a frame audit, and the frame error (a factor of four against every literature constant, originating in THEOREM13.md's false sentence that the two deformations share Lambda exactly) sat in a file already marked verified. Agreement between two routes sharing the whole evaluator (independence radius 9 of 12 layers, measured only after the order) was being read as independence.", "missing_capability": "A mandatory pre-headline gate for any new named constant: a FRAME section stating the normalization against the nearest published frame with a numerically checked conversion, and an independence radius measured with harness/independence.py rather than asserted, both before the first status line.", "category": "domain-judgment", "automatability": "designed", "caught_by": "human", "automation_evidence": "", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "The next hunt that lands a constant carries FRAME.md and a measured independence radius before its first headline, without being told. The gate now exists as lambda_dh_bounds/GATE.md and the closure workflow; harness/independence.py predated the session and was not invoked until ordered.", "notes": "The gate found two false sentences a reader would quote, one genuine blind spot (M2, wrong-answer onset at deflation 75 versus guard at 55.7, ordering luck not structure), and a prior-art displacement (Bombieri-Ghosh 2011 owns the exact zero abscissa sigma_0 only bounds). The mathematics survived; the sentences did not."}
{"kind": "intervention", "session": "2026-08-16/math-conjecture-advancement", "date": "2026-08-16", "what_required_intervention": "After the gate closed YES, the operator judged the novelty statement weaker than the evidence supported and ordered a creative re-read. The strongest claim (Lambda_DH > Lambda_zeta, via Lambda_zeta <= 0.22 from Polymath 15 against the decided wide-frame floor 0.2304, the first strict order between de Bruijn-Newman constants of two Dirichlet series) was already computed and sitting in FRAME.md as a calibration footnote.", "why_the_system_stopped": "The machinery optimizes claims downward under adversarial pressure: every verification pass weakened language to survive attack, and nothing in it asks the dual question, which stronger statement do the decided numbers already support. The separation needed no new computation, only the recognition that a decided number crosses a published bound.", "missing_capability": "A consequence-mining pass after a gate closes: enumerate order relations between the new decided numbers and every cited literature constant in the record, and flag strict crossings as candidate claims for a human to judge.", "category": "domain-judgment", "automatability": "speculative", "caught_by": "human", "automation_evidence": "", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "A pass over an existing hunt's results.json plus its cited constants that surfaces a crossing a human then judges worth claiming, on a hunt where nobody pointed at it first.", "notes": "The elevation is running under its own adversary (does anyone order two such constants anywhere) before the claim is adopted; recognition and verification stay separated."}
{"kind": "observation", "session": "2026-08-16/math-conjecture-advancement", "date": "2026-08-16", "observation": "All three interventions in this session arrived between one-shot workflow passes, never during one. The pattern each time: a full pass runs to a confident artifact, the operator redirects in one or two sentences, a second full pass repairs or extends. Each redirect moved the result materially (a false novelty claim killed; a factor-of-four frame error surfaced with two quotable false sentences; the headline claim strengthened at zero compute cost). The steering cost is tiny against the pass cost, but the machinery only accepts steering at pass boundaries: nothing inside a running workflow consumes an operator nudge mid-flight. Two sibling collisions the same day (BOMBIERI-GHOSH.md landing mid-task under another agent; a docstring left unrepaired because it belonged to a concurrent closure item) show sessions improvising the coordination the operating plane does not provide.", "arose_from": "hunts/prime_zeta_rightmost (Hunt #35) and hunts/lambda_dh_bounds (Hunt #36), 2026-08-16: the prior-art adjudication, the DH NEWMAN CONSTANT GATE, and the separation elevation.", "supports": "meta/operator-functions.md's decomposition: the three interventions are skepticism routing, severity calibration, and scope steering, in that order, and none was produced by machinery.", "undercuts": "The implicit operating assumption that a hunt is a one-shot unit of work. On this evidence a hunt is a sequence of passes whose value concentrates at the boundaries where judgment enters, and an operating plane that only launches hunts, without cheap nudge points between passes, leaves its highest-leverage input unspent."}
{"session": "2026-08-22/rick-chroma-hue", "date": "2026-08-22", "what_required_intervention": "Operator asked whether the laboratory had already done what the session was presenting as new. It had: hunt #76 hand-rolled a numpy re-measurement of zeta.explicit.prime_spectrum, whose docstring states the measured identity outright, and reported the composite control across several turns as a finding.", "why_the_system_stopped": "Nothing checked the hunt's declared frontier against this repository. The session searched the outside literature twice and credited it correctly, and searched the tree zero times. It regenerated CONTEXT.md, the index that lists prime_spectrum, twice without reading it. The HuntSpec frontier field was filled in by the hunt about itself, which is the one direction in which a self-declaration is worthless.", "missing_capability": "A prior-art check of a hunt's subject against the repository's own generated index, run before the hunt writes its frontier rather than after it publishes.", "category": "automatable", "automatability": "speculative", "caught_by": "human", "automation_evidence": "", "evidence_that_would_demonstrate": "A hunt opening with a frontier that cites an in-tree symbol the author had not already read, or a correction notice raised by something other than the operator asking.", "notes": "This session was launched outside fulcrum, so no brief supplied the frontier. That explains this one failure and none of the others: the index, the grep and list_sessions were all available regardless of launch path, and list_sessions was skipped while repository state was described to the operator repeatedly. Countermeasure landed is a correction notice on docs/34 plus tests/test_zeta_temperament.py::test_prime_spectrum_already_computes_this_hunts_measurement, which pins the duplication so it cannot be quietly forgotten. No new tooling was built, per harness/VERDICT.md.", "kind": "intervention"}
